**Read:** [Planning and interpreting XPS — Baer and colleagues](../sources/XPS-Planning-Interpretation-and-Reporting-Baer.lingua#r_6CY69TZ791W5PW0SZCY536SVH5), specimen preparation and compatibility; [Nonvolatile residue collection and analysis — Gordon, Rutherford and Aranda](../sources/Nonvolatile-Residue-Collection-and-Analysis-NASA.lingua#r_2YMCRSWR07NCHECM0X4D8FJ6HJ), collection and blanks. Revisit [Blanks, recovery and the origin of a signal](../evidence/Blanks-recovery-and-the-origin-of-a-signal.lingua#r_3NHG85VQ6ZKJCQ5XSRT60FKM74) and [Nonvolatile residue is defined by an analytical operation](../cleaning/Nonvolatile-residue-is-defined-by-an-analytical-operation.lingua#r_1QVN0HZS2ACY1X3HQ3MAFX6KNN).

Handling, transport, storage, vacuum exposure and the measuring probe can change a surface. A solvent may remove the material of interest; exposure to air may create a new layer; a beam can modify a sensitive specimen. Specify the intended state and which transformations separate it from the observation.

Distinguish a product specimen from a witness coupon. A witness can be useful evidence of a shared exposure, but its temperature, orientation, material and location can change deposition or retention. Matching elapsed time alone does not establish equivalent contamination.

Keep a chain of specimen state, location, contact history and elapsed time. A blank follows selected parts of that chain. Compare an untouched region or a second specimen where that is informative; measurement itself may prevent observing the original state again.

**Work:** Draw a surface's history from production through a proposed measurement. Mark which quantities may change at each stage and which observations could reveal the change. For a hypothetical witness beside a product, identify the assumptions needed to use its result as evidence about the product. State the narrower conclusion supported when those assumptions are uncertain.

